Draft a plan from plain words, and hand it over only when a person accepts

Adds the server side of plain-language Start work: a startWorkService whose plan() turns what a board user typed into a reviewable suggest_tasks card, and a POST /companies/:companyId/start-work/plan route mounted right after the starter catalog routes. The service checks, in order, that the request key has not already produced a plan (if it has, the stored container and card come back unchanged with no second AI call, and the left-out list and the recurring note are read back from the plan's own activity row so the header reads exactly as it did the first time), that this person has not asked this company for more than ten fresh plans in the last ten minutes (429 with plain words, kept in memory as a speed bump against a script, and never reached by a repeat of a key because that never calls a model), that the company has at least one agent that is not paused, terminated or waiting for approval (422 before any AI call), and that an AI model is set up (503 before any write). It then builds a prompt from this company's facts only (name, usable agents with role, title and capabilities, ready plugin keys, routine titles, the starter cards and which ones match the words), writes one activity log row before the model is called so a person running the route hard is visible even when every draft then fails, asks the model once through the shared completeOnce helper, and gives it one repair round with the exact problems if the answer is unusable. That repair round covers the parent rules as well as the JSON shape: a task naming a parent no task in the plan has, or a set of tasks sitting under each other in a loop, is quoted back in the same plain form and gets the same one chance to correct itself. The model's answer is made safe before anyone sees it: dashes are stripped, only the whitelisted task fields survive, an assignee outside the roster is swapped for the company's lead, any task needing a plugin that is not installed and running is listed under Left out with the same words the starter cards use (and never reaches the card), and the parent tree is checked with the same rule accept uses. Concurrent submits of one key in one process share a single promise, and a second container from another process trips the 0097 unique index and hands back the first. Only then does one transaction write the container issue (backlog, nobody assigned, originKind start_work, originId = request key, title Request: first line, the operator's words as description) and the pending card (idempotency key start-work:<key>, wake_assignee_on_accept, footer on every task naming the request identifier), and in that same transaction one more activity log row holding the facts a redisplay needs (the left-out list, whether it sounded recurring, and the recurring note), so re-sending the same request key returns the same header rather than a blank one. A third row is written after the transaction for the card itself. The response carries every header fact the dialog will show (lead, budget warnings, outbound approval hold from instance settings, model used, recurring note, portfolio root flag, matched starter cards). Also exports buildTaskCreationOrder and adds a serviceUnavailable error helper so nothing is copied. 27 new tests (18 service, 9 route) cover all of it against embedded Postgres and supertest.

When a board user accepts the plan card on a Start work container (an issue with originKind start_work), the server now hands the request over in the same transaction as the once-only claim and the child tasks: the container moves from backlog to todo, is assigned to the company's lead picked by pickAssigneeForCompany at accept time (CEO, then an officer, then any agent that is not paused, terminated or waiting for approval, or nobody if the roster has changed), and its description gains an 'Accepted plan' section listing every created task identifier plus any tasks the reviewer left out. acceptSuggestedTasks returns that as continuationIssue, which the existing accept route already passes to the continuation wake and logs as issue.updated; the log source is now keyed by kind, so request confirmations keep 'request_confirmation_accept' and plans get 'start_work_accept'. Because the hook runs after the claim and after every child exists, a 409 or a failed child creation leaves the container in backlog and unassigned, so no agent can be woken on a request whose plan was not accepted. Rejecting that same card now runs in a transaction and cancels the container, with no wake. Both hooks fire only for the card that is the request's own plan: the card's idempotency key has to be the one minted for this request key, and the container has to still be in backlog with nobody assigned. Once the plan is accepted the lead keeps working on the container, and a card the lead suggests later lands on that same issue; such a card is treated exactly like a card on any ordinary issue, so accepting it cannot hand the request over a second time (which would reset the status, replace the assignee and drop the running agent's lock) and rejecting it cannot cancel the whole request out from under its live children. suggest_tasks cards on ordinary issues behave exactly as before; eight new service tests against embedded Postgres and one new route test pin all of this.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
